## Representability-aware pre-apply validation — 2026-08-30

The pre-apply gate now distinguishes structural blockers from style findings that can safely remain review notes. Every mapper finding carries its original `code`, `severity`, `blocking`, `surface`, `viewport`, `message`, and remediation guidance. A style-only finding such as box shadow, asymmetric border, non-default box sizing, or a non-simple background is classified as a non-blocking `Review note [custom-css]`; it no longer changes the entire section to `unclassified`. The native node can therefore proceed while the residual visual difference remains explicit for Custom CSS review.

Structural uncertainty remains blocking. Unclassified layouts, missing layout evidence, transformed or overlapping children, responsive layout-mode transitions that need an explicit decision, and unresolved local assets continue to prevent Apply. Classifier diagnostics such as `absolute-or-transformed-child` are preserved instead of being hidden behind the generic `layout-unclassified` message. Target validation labels these items as `Blocking [layout]`, `Blocking [asset-mapping]`, or the relevant surface and displays the source section/viewport.

Custom JavaScript is intentionally not used as a layout or styling fallback. It remains reserved for published runtime behavior. The target report now exposes both `structuralErrors` and `reviewNotes`; only blocking errors participate in `canApply`, while review notes remain visible in the same pre-apply report.

